// ===== Wegovy case (second case study) =====
DD_GUIDE.push({id:"g-case2", lec:"Case study", t:"Wegovy (semaglutide) case", lo:["P1-12"], pts:[
  {x:"**Wegovy** = semaglutide, a **GLP-1 receptor agonist** approved for **weight loss** (same molecule as Ozempic, different indication and dose — **2.4 mg** weekly). Made by **recombinant DNA**; peptide backbone by **yeast fermentation**."},
  {x:"Structure changes vs human GLP-1 (**94% homology**): **Ala8 → Aib** (α-aminoisobutyric acid) → resists **DPP-4** · **C18 fatty di-acid on Lys26 via a glutamate spacer** → **albumin binding** → long-acting, once weekly · **Lys34 → Arg** → stops the fatty acid attaching at the wrong site."},
  {x:"MOA: GLP-1 is a physiological regulator of **appetite and caloric intake**; GLP-1 receptors sit in brain areas for appetite. In animals semaglutide **reached and activated neurons in brain regions that regulate food intake**."},
  {x:"Preclinical: **thyroid C-cell tumors** in mice and rats (dose- and duration-dependent, lifetime exposure); human relevance unknown → **black box warning** for thyroid C-cell tumors."},
  {x:"**Phase I:** **30 subjects with obesity**, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, **two-period crossover**; dose-escalated to 1.0 mg weekly. Lower energy intake at lunch (−1255 kJ), evening meal and snacks; less hunger/cravings, less preference for high-fat food; **−5.0 kg** body weight."},
  {x:"**Phase II:** dose-ranging, **957 adults without diabetes, BMI ≥ 30**, 71 sites, 8 countries, **52 weeks**, 9 arms (semaglutide 0.05–0.4 mg, liraglutide 3.0 mg, placebo). Weight loss −2.3% placebo vs up to **−13.8% (0.4 mg)**; ≥0.2 mg beat **liraglutide** (−7.8%). Main AEs: dose-related **GI**."},
  {x:"**Phase III:** 129 sites, 16 countries, **68 weeks**, **semaglutide 2.4 mg (n = 1,306) vs placebo (n = 655)**. Weight **−14.9% vs −2.4%** (−15.3 kg vs −2.6 kg); **86.4% vs 31.5%** lost ≥ 5%. Most common AEs: **nausea, diarrhea**."},
  {x:"Case answers to remember: cost **~$200M–$1B**, **10–15 years**. Phase IV: FDA **can require** it; company **must monitor side effects** after launch (more people → more side effects). New indication = **full clinical trials** again (what Novo did going from Ozempic to Wegovy)."}
]});

const WEGOVY_QS = [
 {q:"What is the source of Wegovy?", a:"Active ingredient **semaglutide** (GLP-1 agonist), made by **recombinant DNA technology**; the peptide backbone is produced by **yeast fermentation**."},
 {q:"What is the mechanism of action of Wegovy?", a:"GLP-1 analogue (**94% homology**) that **selectively binds and activates the GLP-1 receptor**. GLP-1 regulates **appetite and caloric intake**; GLP-1 receptors are in brain areas for appetite, and in animals semaglutide **activated neurons in brain regions that regulate food intake**."},
 {q:"Is the mechanism of action of Wegovy unique?", a:"It's a **long-acting** GLP-1 agonist: **Ala8 → Aib** (resists DPP-4), **C18 fatty acid on Lys26 via glutamate spacer** (albumin binding), **Lys34 → Arg** (fatty acid can't bind at the wrong site)."},
 {q:"In Phase I, normal human subjects or patients? Why?", a:"**Usually normal healthy subjects**, to evaluate **safety, tolerability, PK and PD**. But it can be done in the **target population** to look at efficacy — Wegovy's Phase I used **subjects with obesity**."},
 {q:"How many subjects would you use in Phase I?", a:"Usually **20–100**; the Wegovy Phase I trial had **~30** people."},
 {q:"Would you recommend the drug for Phase II?", a:"**It depends on the toxic dose** — if the drug has a **wide safety margin**, recommend Phase II."},
 {q:"For Phase II, patients or volunteers, and how many?", a:"**Patients**, usually **100–300** — more with more arms. Wegovy's Phase II had **957 patients in 9 arms** (semaglutide doses, liraglutide 3.0 mg, placebo)."},
 {q:"If some Phase II subjects show complete/partial response, would you recommend Phase III?", a:"**Depends** on the %, and whether it's **statistically significantly better than placebo or the standard of care**; also re-check whether **side effects are significant or manageable** in a larger population."},
 {q:"For Phase III, patients or volunteers, and how many?", a:"**Patients** — usually **hundreds to thousands** (Wegovy: 1,306 semaglutide + 655 placebo)."},
 {q:"If the drug shows good response with relatively low risk, what step does the sponsor take?", a:"File a **New Drug Application (NDA)**."},
 {q:"At what stage does the sponsor file the IND?", a:"**Before any human testing.**"},
 {q:"As an FDA reviewer, what do you expect in the IND vs the NDA?", a:"**IND:** preclinical, **CMC**, **clinical protocol**. **NDA:** preclinical, CMC, **clinical trial data**, manufacturing and **labeling**."},
 {q:"Is the sponsor required to do additional studies?", a:"**Phase IV if they want.** To extend the molecule to a **new use**, they must run the **whole set of clinical trials** again — like Novo did for semaglutide (Ozempic → Wegovy)."},
 {q:"After NDA approval, must the sponsor do more studies or monitor side effects? Why?", a:"**FDA can require Phase IV**, and the company can choose to do one. The company **must monitor side effects** because once on the market **many more people use it**, so more side effects may appear."},
 {q:"As CEO, how much money and how long?", a:"About **$200 million to $1 billion** and **10–15 years**."}
];

