// ===== PHAR 117 — Pharmaceutical Chemistry (ochem) content (from study guide + practice exam) =====
const OC_GUIDE = [
 {id:"o-bond", t:"Bonding & structure", pts:[
  {x:"**Valence electrons** = outer-shell electrons; they form bonds and decide how an atom bonds and reacts (C 4, N 5, O 6, halogens 7)."},
  {x:"**Hybridization:** **sp³** = tetrahedral, 109.5°, 4 σ bonds (alkanes) · **sp²** = trigonal planar, 120°, 3 σ + 1 π (alkenes, C=O, aromatics, amide) · **sp** = linear, 180°, 2 σ + 2 π (alkynes, nitriles C≡N)."},
  {x:"**Sigma (σ) bonds**: overlap of sp^x and/or s orbitals → **free rotation → flexibility**."},
  {x:"**Pi (π) bonds**: overlap of **unhybridized p orbitals** on sp² or sp atoms → **no free rotation → rigidity**. π electrons are **higher energy and more reactive** than σ electrons."}
 ]},
 {id:"o-imf", t:"Bond polarity & intermolecular forces", pts:[
  {x:"A bond is **polar** when the electronegativity difference is **≥ 0.5**. Partial charges let polar bonds join stronger interactions (opposites attract)."},
  {x:"**Strongest → weakest:** **Ion–ion** > **ion–dipole** > **dipole–dipole** (incl. H-bonds) > **dipole–induced dipole** > **instantaneous dipole** (London/van der Waals)."},
  {x:"**Ion–ion**: two full charges (e.g., ionized carboxylate –COO⁻ with protonated amine –NH₃⁺; salt bridge). **Ion–dipole**: ion + polar group (ionized drug dissolving in water). **Dipole–dipole / H-bond**: polar groups (O–H, N–H, C=O); water is strong dipole–dipole. **Dipole–induced dipole**: polar group polarizes a nonpolar one (e.g., aromatic ring). **Instantaneous dipole**: nonpolar–nonpolar (alkane chains)."},
  {x:"Polar bonds: dipole–dipole, ion–dipole, dipole–induced dipole. **Nonpolar bonds: van der Waals only.** These forces decide receptor recognition and how a drug can be formulated."}
 ]},
 {id:"o-np", t:"Nonpolar functional groups", pts:[
  {x:"**Alkanes**: only C–C and C–H σ bonds → flexible, tetrahedral, **nonpolar, lipophilic/hydrophobic** (partition into membranes). Relatively unreactive but can be **oxidized by CYP450** (ibuprofen example). In excipients: waxes, oils, soft gels, **oil-based IM vehicles** for steroids, **triglyceride (fatty acid ester) suppository bases**."},
  {x:"**Haloalkanes (C–X)**: C–F/C–Cl can be polar, but **adding halogens always increases hydrophobicity**. Halogen on an **alkane ↑** CYP oxidation of that carbon; halogen on an **aromatic ring ↓/blocks** CYP oxidation. **Primary haloalkanes react by SN2** → rare in drugs (exception: **mechlorethamine**). Highly halogenated small molecules = **volatile anesthetics** (halothane, isoflurane, sevoflurane…)."},
  {x:"**Alkenes (C=C)**: 1 σ + 1 π → **rigid, planar**, **more reactive** than alkanes (π electrons). Prone to **oxidation and light** reactions. Oxidized unsaturated fats → **small carboxylic acids = rancid smell**. Nonpolar. Vitamin A = conjugated alkenes, light-sensitive."},
  {x:"**Thiol (C–S–H) / thioether (C–S–C)**: **nonpolar, lipophilic** (S ≈ C, H in EN), flexible. Sulfur is **oxidized**: thiols → **disulfides**; thioethers → **sulfoxide → sulfone**. S is large and **polarizable → good nucleophile**. **Captopril's thiol binds the zinc in ACE.** Thiols are used as **antioxidants**."}
 ]},
 {id:"o-p", t:"Polar functional groups", pts:[
  {x:"**Alcohol (C–O–H)**: polar; **H-bond donor AND acceptor**; adds hydrophilicity. Excipients: **cosolvents** (liquids), bulking agents, binders, disintegrants. **Starch** (many OH) = tablet **diluent + disintegrant**."},
  {x:"**Ether (C–O–C)**: polar, **H-bond acceptor only** (no donor) → **less polar than alcohol**; flexible. **PEG** = cosolvent, tablet coating, **hydrophilic suppository base**."},
  {x:"**Carbonyl (C=O)** in ketones, aldehydes, acids, esters, amides: polar, **H-bond acceptor**, open to **nucleophilic attack**; can undergo **keto–enol tautomerization**."},
  {x:"**Carboxylic acid (–COOH)**: ↑ water solubility, **much more when ionized/salt** (naproxen **sodium**). **Ester (–COOR)**: less soluble (no H donor, can't ionize). **Esters hydrolyze** (acid, base, **esterases**) → 'disposable' → **prodrugs**, taste masking, better absorption. **Low-MW esters = flavorings.**"},
  {x:"**Amide (–CONR₂)**: **more stable to hydrolysis than esters** (peptidases can cleave). Donor if N–H. **Rigid — no rotation about N–C=O** (resonance gives partial double bond). Essential to proteins. **Povidone** (cyclic amide/lactam polymer) = water-soluble diluent + disintegrant."},
  {x:"**Amine (R₃N)**: H-bond acceptor; donor if N–H. **Acts as a base when the lone pair is free** to take H⁺. Lone pair tied up (amide N, pyrrole N, aniline delocalized) → weak/not basic."},
  {x:"**Reactive groups** (degrade by water, O₂, light, nucleophiles): **esters, primary haloalkanes, β-lactams, epoxides, alkenes**."}
 ]},
 {id:"o-ab", t:"Acid/base & Henderson–Hasselbalch", pts:[
  {x:"Acid = **donates H⁺**; base = **accepts H⁺**. Acids: carboxylic acids, phenols, sulfonamides, imides. Bases: amines, amidines/guanidines, imidazoles, pyridines."},
  {x:"**Acidic drug:** HA = **molecular acid** (unionized, **absorbable**) ⇌ A⁻ = **conjugate base** (ionized, **excretable**)."},
  {x:"**Basic drug:** B = **free base** (unionized, **absorbable**) ⇌ BH⁺ = **conjugate acid** (ionized, **excretable**)."},
  {x:"**pKa = −log Ka.** Lower pKa = stronger acid. For bases, pKa refers to the conjugate acid: **higher pKa = stronger base** (amines 10–11 > amidines 9–10 > imidazole ~5 > pyrrole 1–3 per practice exam)."},
  {x:"**pH = −log[H⁺]**; lower pH = more H⁺ = more acidic."},
  {x:"**HH:** acid: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]); base: pH = pKa + log([B]/[BH⁺]). Shortcut: 1 unit away → 90:10; 2 units → 99:1; 3 → 99.9:0.1."},
  {x:"Rule: **acids ionize at high pH** (conjugate base dominates); **bases ionize at low pH** (conjugate acid dominates). Unionized form crosses membranes."}
 ]},
 {id:"o-sol", t:"Solubility & log P", pts:[
  {x:"Solubility factors: functional groups (polarity, H-bonding, ionization), **molecular shape/size**, crystal packing/melting point (**polymorphism**), **pH vs pKa**, temperature."},
  {x:"Hydrophilicity (most → least, roughly): ionized groups (–COO⁻, –NH₃⁺) > OH / NH / COOH > amide > ester, ketone, ether > halogens, alkanes."},
  {x:"**log P** = log([drug]octanol / [drug]water) — lipophilicity of the **unionized** drug. Drug-like range ≈ **0–5** (Lipinski: ≤ 5). log D accounts for ionization at a given pH."},
  {x:"**Hydrophobic effect**: nonpolar groups cluster to release ordered water → driven by an **increase in entropy**."},
  {x:"**Isoelectric point (pI)**: pH where an amphoteric molecule has **net zero charge** → lowest solubility."},
  {x:"Ways to increase aqueous solubility (check your class notes for the exact '3 general ways'): **pH adjustment / salt formation**, **cosolvents** (alcohols, PEG), and **chemical modification** (add polar groups/prodrug) — also surfactants, complexation."},
  {x:"**Equations:** S_T = S₀(1 + Ka/[H⁺]) · acid: **pH = pKa + log((S_T − S₀)/S₀)** · base: **pH = pKa + log(S₀/(S_T − S₀))**. S₀ = intrinsic solubility of the unionized form; S_T = total solubility needed."},
  {x:"**Polymorphism** = two or more crystal forms of the same compound (different solubility/stability). **Amorphous** = no crystal order."}
 ]},
 {id:"o-misc", t:"Drug design & excipient facts", pts:[
  {x:"**Pharmacophore** = the shared arrangement of functional groups that drugs in one class need for activity."},
  {x:"**Bioisostere** = a functional group mimic with similar size, geometry and properties (e.g., **–CF₂H mimics –OH** as an H-bond donor)."},
  {x:"**CYP oxidation targets:** alkane C–H, **thioether sulfur → sulfoxide** (cimetidine), aromatic rings (blocked by halogens)."},
  {x:"Suppositories: **hydrophobic drug in a fatty base (cocoa butter)** stays in the base → **slow release**; PEG/glycerinated gelatin = water-soluble bases."},
  {x:"Rancid smell = **oxidation**. Esters in vivo = **hydrolysis**. Cosolvents usually contain **alcohol** groups."}
 ]}
];

